---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { elsewhere } from "../data/projects";
---

<Base
  title="About, Benjamin Goldberger"
  description="Indie developer building and running small software products alone."
  path="/about"
>
  <a class="back" href="/">← work</a>

  <h1>About</h1>

  <div class="bio-head">
    <img
      class="portrait"
      src="/ben.jpg"
      srcset="/ben.jpg 440w, /ben@2x.jpg 880w"
      sizes="(max-width: 620px) 160px, 200px"
      width="440"
      height="440"
      alt="Benjamin Goldberger"
      loading="eager"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <p class="lede">
      I am an indie developer. I build small software products, put them online, and run
      them myself.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      That means the whole job, not the fun part of it. The code, yes, but also the pricing
      page, the refund emails, the analytics that say nobody came, and the slow work of
      figuring out why. I like that the loop is short and that nothing gets decided by a
      committee.
    </p>

    <p>
      The work splits roughly in two. Some of it is meant to earn: an AI coaching bot on
      subscription, a QR generator sold once instead of rented forever, a print shop attached
      to a content site. The rest exists because I wanted it to exist, which is its own good
      reason. A reverse image search tool with an EXIF parser I wrote from scratch, mostly to
      find out whether I could.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>

    <p>
      Small, cheap to run, and finished. I would rather ship a narrow thing that works than
      keep a broad thing at ninety percent forever. Most of what I make is a static site and
      a small backend, hosted for a few dollars a month, and that constraint has made the
      work better rather than worse.
    </p>

    <p>
      I use AI heavily and say so plainly. It changed which part of the job is hard: the
      typing got cheap, and deciding what is actually true got expensive. So I spend the time
      on the data model, the requirements, and checking the claims, because those are the
      parts a model still does badly.
    </p>

    <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="links">
    {elsewhere.map((l) => <a href={l.href} rel="noopener">{l.label}</a>)}
  </div>
</Base>
